{
	title: 'Card verbosity: short and long; /cards; spinner',
	spec: `Every card that expands and collapses has one of two verbosity states, named the same in code, config.ason and commands: short and long. In the terminal, short is cardVerbosityShortRows rows, the title row included, with no blank row under the title (done, the last row says '… N more lines' at its right; at 1, a tool title's status counts the hidden output: '(13 lines, 1.9s)'). On the web, short is its title row alone; the web gets none of the Bash layout below, since motion on a phone distracts.

A short Bash card (client/bash-card.ts) shows its command in the output color: after the title when it fits beside a status reserve, so its place is the same running and done; else at 1 and 2 rows on the title row anyway, and from 3 rows on its own row. A one-line command too long for its room scrolls once at 200 columns per second from its start to its end (… marks cut ends), timed from the call's ts, so only a card that appears live moves; cards from history show the end. A multi-line command never scrolls: first line ↵ … ↵ last line on one row (↵ in the card's own color), the first line cut in its middle before the last is cut; from 6 rows, first and last lines on two rows. Without a description, a one-line command is the title and is not repeated. Declared files follow while a row stays for output. Output fills the remaining rows and grows as it arrives; a silent command has no output rows. Long shows everything.

Kinds and their config.ason defaults (common/settings.ts, flat keys so /config lists and sets them): cardVerbosityPrompt long (the user's prompts), cardVerbosityText long (replies), cardVerbosityThinking short, cardVerbosityTool short (tool calls with their results), cardVerbosityFailed short (a call whose result is an error or whose bash exit is not 0), cardVerbosityMessage short (another session's message or report, and send and spawn calls), cardVerbosityNotice short (background command reports, prompt-file change cards, Hal's own prompts), cardVerbosityCommit short; cardVerbosityShortRows 3. A message queued for the turn end stays its one-row queue card. common/card.ts maps an item to its kind and start state.

/cards [kind state|kind default|reset] shows or changes the current session's overrides. They are session metadata (meta.blocks), saved and synced to every client; a fork inherits them. A hal/tool session starts with tool long: its calls are what the user typed. A card's start state is the session's override, else the client's own setting (the terminal reads config.ason; the web reads it from the page it was served). Stored values Hal does not know never make a session unreadable: an unknown override reads as the setting, and an unknown setting value as its default, with a warning. Ctrl-O, /toggle, /expand, /collapse and a click on a web card act on top, per client: one named card switches between short and long; /expand sets long and /collapse short; a group becomes long when at least half are short, else short.

Nothing jumps: a short card keeps its height while it streams and when it ends: it shows the last rows while streaming and the first once done. A running tool's short card lays out running output as its short result will be (task 4y4). A running web tool card keeps its state. Long cards grow as text streams; only they have the Hal cursor. Thinking has a title row like replies ('15:41 Thinking', then its text).

Activity: a streaming thinking or reply card and a running tool call show, before the card id, their elapsed time from 1 s, '(12s)', and a one-cell spinner (frames ⠁⠈⠐⠠⢀⡀⠄⠂, one per pulse beat in the terminal, so no extra timer; a CSS animation on the web, a still dot under reduced motion). Once done the cell goes, so the id sits one space after the status.

Tool titles start with the tool as a word: 'Bash <purpose>'; headlines that start with their verb (Read, Edit, Write) keep it.

The web follows the same states, defaults and overrides: a click on a card's header switches it.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Decisions taken without the user (session 172-ink): spinner frames are the dot orbit from the 172-jaw brainstorm; the terminal advances it on the existing 250 ms pulse, so a full orbit takes 2 s and no timer is added. Thinking in glimpse and long has its own title row (it used to flow from the time, task 77f) so the elapsed time and spinner have a fixed place. A nonzero bash exit counts as failed (wm0 notes: most print an error). send and spawn calls are messages. hal/tool sessions start with tool long. Queued one-row cards and whole-output commands (/help) step short and long only. The web glimpse clamps text with CSS and shows no count; running web tools no longer open after a second. No config-example.ason: /config lists every setting and documents what it writes (task tj2).',
		'Terminal modules: tool calls and results moved from item-view.ts to tool-view.ts, and frame.ref became itemView.place, to keep both under 400 lines; block-view.ts holds glimpse, tail and spinner. card.initial needs the failed flag from the result, so start states come from toggle.starts (one scan per use) or, in frame.layout, from the next item. The web Card reads bash() inside a memo that runs at once: declare it before level() (a TDZ error halted Solid otherwise).',
		"Bug fix (2026-10-12, session 172-mag): a running Bash card's short row showed its latest output line with raw tabs (rg output of tab-indented source). clipVisual counts a tab to the next 4-column stop but keeps it; the terminal moved to its own 8-column stop past the pad, so the row overflowed, wrapped, and every row below drew one line lower than the renderer believed (duplicated title and tab-bar rows). Invariant: no terminal row holds a raw tab; a row clipped with clipVisual passes through strings.expandTabs, as closedRow does.",
		'strings.expandTabs documents four-column per-line stops, preserved ANSI escapes, expansion before display measurement/clipping/wrapping and padding, repaint corruption from raw terminal tabs, and retention of original text for storage/editing offsets. clipVisual explicitly documents that it preserves raw tabs and directs terminal callers to expandTabs.',
		'New hal/tool sessions start with blocks { tool: long, failed: long }: failed calls open like the others. Existing sessions keep their stored blocks.',
		'/model hal/tool merges sessions.toolBlocks into the session blocks, so switched tabs open calls and failures like new hal/tool sessions. Switching away keeps them.',
		"Stored session.ason blocks may still hold the retired level 'glimpse'; sessions.validate accepts it and card.verbosity reads it as short. Rejecting it emptied the tab bar (14 Oct).",
		'Two states (user, 2026-10-14): short and long only; the middle state and the extra prompt/tool state are gone. Ctrl-O p still expands draft pastes. Invariant restored: a stored value Hal does not know (an old session override or setting) never makes a session unreadable; sessions.ts checks only that blocks is an object, card.verbosity reads an unknown override as the setting, and settings.check an unknown value as the default with a warning. Old session.ason files keep their stored values.',
		'Short Bash layout (user, 2026-10-15): while a command scrolls, appView sets view.now and the pulse runs at pulse.frame (50 ms); frame.scrolls keeps that card out of the reused prefix and in its row-cache key. The call records its rows before output in toolView.heads (a WeakMap on the call item) and frame passes them to the attached result as look.head, so live and finished output fill the same rows.'
	],
	needs: ['c5x', 'wm0', 'ghs', 'r4d', 'v8y', 'fn', 'pc'],
